{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-332607" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above and its trajectory passing through the point $(-1,2)$ in the phase space of the system. What is the tangent vector to the trajectory at this point?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332607" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nIn our case, the trajectory (solution) passes through the point $(x,y)=(-1,2)$ in the phase space. Substituting the coordinates of this point into the equations of our system, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =-3x+y \\\\  & =-3(-1)+2 \\\\  & =3+2 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =2x+4y \\\\  & =2(-1)+4(2) \\\\  & =-2+8 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent vector to the trajectory is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332607" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2ba6ec0ff0bd36366ee3365452b59a030a352a8435f5099b4a23a938cf89a68c"} {"question" "q-332607" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332607" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332607" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332616" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,x(t)={e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332616" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & ={e}^{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+16[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 48\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 48\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 48\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2(4)+0(48) \\\\ 0(4)+4(48)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 192\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332616" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2ba6ec0ff0bd36366ee3365452b59a030a352a8435f5099b4a23a938cf89a68c"} {"question" "q-332616" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332616" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}192 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 48\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 192\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 48\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332616" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 192\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332614" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above and its trajectory passing through the point $(0,-2)$ in the phase space of the system. What is the tangent vector to the trajectory at this point?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332614" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nIn our case, the trajectory (solution) passes through the point $(x,y)=(0,-2)$ in the phase space. Substituting the coordinates of this point into the equations of our system, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =3x-2y \\\\  & =3(0)-2(-2) \\\\  & =0+4 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-4x+y \\\\  & =-4(0)+(-2) \\\\  & =0-2 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent vector to the trajectory is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332614" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2ba6ec0ff0bd36366ee3365452b59a030a352a8435f5099b4a23a938cf89a68c"} {"question" "q-332614" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332614" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332614" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332619" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,x(t)={e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332619" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & ={e}^{-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2(\\frac{1}{3})+0(6) \\\\ 0(\\frac{1}{3})+1(6)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332619" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2ba6ec0ff0bd36366ee3365452b59a030a352a8435f5099b4a23a938cf89a68c"} {"question" "q-332619" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332619" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332619" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above and its trajectory passing through the point $(-1,-2)$ in the phase space of the system. What is the tangent vector to the trajectory at this point?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nIn our case, the trajectory (solution) passes through the point $(x,y)=(-1,-2)$ in the phase space. Substituting the coordinates of this point into the equations of our system, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =2x+y \\\\  & =2(-1)+(-2) \\\\  & =-2-2 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-x+3y \\\\  & =-(-1)+3(-2) \\\\  & =1-6 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent vector to the trajectory is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2ba6ec0ff0bd36366ee3365452b59a030a352a8435f5099b4a23a938cf89a68c"} {"question" "q-332612" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332612" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332612" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332660" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space.$\\,$\nThe $x$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332660" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that: \n\n- The $x$-nullcline is a line for which ${x}^{′}=0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are vertical). So, to find it, we set the first equation of the system equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n2x-y=0\\,⇒\\,y=2x\n$$\n\n- The $y$-nullcline is a line for which ${y}^{′}=0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are horizontal). So, to find it, we set the second equation of the system equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n-x-3y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-\\frac{1}{3}x\n$$\n\nPlotting these in the phase space, we obtain the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/a9c0fb2365806ddb02ff8c60a618aabf4cc825917c29fb472ee3bab2492b9033.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332660" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2ba6ec0ff0bd36366ee3365452b59a030a352a8435f5099b4a23a938cf89a68c"} {"question" "q-332660" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332660" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2x"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}x"] ["math" "2x"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332660" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332660" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332660" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3x"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{3}x"] ["math" "3x"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332660" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{3}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332662" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space.\n$\\,$\nThe $x$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332662" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that: \n\n- The $x$-nullcline is a line for which ${x}^{′}=0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are vertical). So, to find it, we set the first equation of the system equal to $0:$\n\n$$\nx+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-x\n$$\n\n- The $y$-nullcline is a line for which ${y}^{′}=0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are horizontal). So, to find it, we set the second equation of the system equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n-4x+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=4x\n$$\n\nPlotting these in the phase space, we obtain the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/e1d157e7c921aa6fd2dd5449934baf0258dc5a17fc9f6e6db672a783d59553ca.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332662" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2ba6ec0ff0bd36366ee3365452b59a030a352a8435f5099b4a23a938cf89a68c"} {"question" "q-332662" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-work