rse_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/14ed16cc149ae777e195aa59e39cf809e939032a925e7acbd88cd685af99dd0e.png)\n\nA sketch of the phase space with nullclines and the corresponding direction field of tangents to the solutions of a system of differential equations is shown above (the tangents are not drawn to scale). Which of the following could represent the trajectory starting at the point marked on the graph?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's sketch the trajectory.\n\n- We start at the given point and go along with the flow (straight to the right) until the trajectory intersects the $y$-nullcline, where the tangents are horizontal.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/0d010be8bad72bf4ebac4dbe2d325f1a1f194d78c676e2a62306b5c7d2af3c66.png)\n\n- Next, the flow continues going to the right  but then starts turning downward.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/5118424696b9602f10bf116c3a292e2fb58ed2f9acd2a9507888ab92a9b8986b.png)\n\n- After that, the trajectory continues to move rightward and bend downward, converging to the origin.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/92a58573efafd7155cf02faf52e27ab968d9161ad448530b45673fd83a61fde1.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2ba6ec0ff0bd36366ee3365452b59a030a352a8435f5099b4a23a938cf89a68c"} {"question" "q-332920" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332920" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/5852fdce770beeaa73192b4487c445a5b6cdd857a01b08465c60c1ffeeba9aed.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/6daec64ca18c09907c43f10f5f2e1b46f693782bddcccb8fccd0240534120100.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/9f32c0e475c4973fed376a2392bad1b159123da933c6defb7145cc6ab6ce2634.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/dffc5c6e0d736e34aae32e10158a1e9ea5071562cd50cc630fe6b82740dfe439.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/e265cd75cfd7cd4178a6d222d86043d97c816d36286b516898d8ff0b0e6d208c.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "q-332920" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/dffc5c6e0d736e34aae32e10158a1e9ea5071562cd50cc630fe6b82740dfe439.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/state.json" "file_sha256" "150f600fed24a0b162119b5e3580d586627bf74d4449048d83c83c5ac600b48a"} {"question" "e-21585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/d8be54eddd0358742170a03b3c55e0cb94eaf2a7d59f12e1c4361a6197d29847.png)\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\ln 2?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/example-21585.json" "file_sha256" "cd58e5d025795c8c1399930d5af84e67bb9a47fdeab3787f0cfd0ffd24d77804"} {"question" "e-21585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\ln 2$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & ={e}^{3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+2{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =8[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+2\\cdot 2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =8[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2(12)+1(4) \\\\ 1(12)+2(4)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}24+4 \\\\ 12+8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}28 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/example-21585.json" "file_sha256" "cd58e5d025795c8c1399930d5af84e67bb9a47fdeab3787f0cfd0ffd24d77804"} {"question" "e-21586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/example-21586.json" "file_sha256" "8c38af3435ad03e4ba02af70e0318f88c80bd2870bf5246326490440231050f5"} {"question" "e-21586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that: \n\n- The $x$-nullcline is a line for which $x' = 0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are vertical). So, to find it, we set the first equation of the system equal to $0{:}$\n\n$$\n3x-y=0 \n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\ny = 3x\n$$\n\n- The $y$-nullcline is a line for which $y' = 0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are horizontal). So, to find it, we set the second equation of the system equal to $0{:}$\n\n$$\n-2x-y=0 \n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\ny = -2x\n$$\n\nPlotting these in the phase space, we obtain the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/66cdd05ac68d762c5b78df40b22f9e0cd660099c13e48e9eb71c81c73a044280.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/example-21586.json" "file_sha256" "8c38af3435ad03e4ba02af70e0318f88c80bd2870bf5246326490440231050f5"} {"question" "e-21588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A sketch of the phase space with nullclines and the corresponding direction field of tangents to the solutions of a system of differential equations is shown below (the tangents are not drawn to scale). Sketch the trajectory of the solution starting at the marked point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/0cf7942e618f0465ead5b98c6404a3747e505db75517725971c833c97121ab64.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/example-21588.json" "file_sha256" "c3871ee034843d3fbf1a4042057abf22b9dc8ffd2b2df7009d895a6099724f99"} {"question" "e-21588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's sketch the trajectory.\n\n- We start at the given point, moving along with the flow (downward and slightly to the right) until the trajectory intersects the $x$-nullcline, where the tangents are vertical.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/105bdbdcac5f0ff20f71a0131bf5bb3cbafe0e93ce8bc733a51c2dcb3fb8364f.png)\n\n- Next, the flow continues downward, but the trajectory starts to turn slightly to the left.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/96757a3aee3b161b2ba9887cd85f977f063807779604647dd791cf265790cabc.png)\n\n- After that, the trajectory continues to go downwards and slightly to the left to infinity.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/658c15d2291368d0e6836ba19b90157d49dee4a510d58cddf7f85ddc9ff72a8d.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/example-21588.json" "file_sha256" "c3871ee034843d3fbf1a4042057abf22b9dc8ffd2b2df7009d895a6099724f99"}] "usage" {"q-332920" [] "e-21588" [] "e-21586" [] "e-21585" []} "basis" 2278 "retractions" [[17592186167166 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3188/Source/Images/q-332920.png)\n\nA sketch of the phase space with nullclines and the corresponding direction field of tangents to the solutions of a system of differential equations is shown above (the tangents are not drawn to scale). Which of the following could represent the trajectory starting at the point marked on the graph?"] [17592186166465 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\ln 2$?"] [17592186166465 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\ln 2$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(\\ln 2) &= e^{3(\\ln 2)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + 2e^{\\ln 2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 8\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + 2 \\cdot 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 8\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + 4\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{′}(\\ln 2) &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2(12) + 1(4) \\\\ 1(12) + 2(4)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}24 + 4 \\\\ 12 + 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}28 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186166466 :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = 3x(t) - y(t); y^{′}(t) =-2x(t) - y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space."] [17592186166466 :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- The $x$-nullcline is a line for which $x' = 0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are vertical). So, to find it, we set the first equation of the system equal to $0{:}$\n$3x-y=0 \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad y = 3x$\n- The $y$-nullcline is a line for which $y' = 0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are horizontal). So, to find it, we set the second equation of the system equal to $0{:}$\n$-2x-y=0 \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad y = -2x$\n\nPlotting these in the phase space, we obtain the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3188/Source/Images/329339a73bbba31c17772216e1329304.png)"] [17592186166467 :question/problem "A sketch of the phase space with nullclines and the corresponding direction field of tangents to the solutions of a system of differential equations is shown below (the tangents are not drawn to scale). Sketch the trajectory of the solution starting at the marked point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3188/Source/Images/d259112c09bd560643afeb7ef35f3134.png)"] [17592186166467 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the trajectory.\n\n- We start at the given point, moving along with the flow (downward and slightly to the right) until the trajectory intersects the $x$-nullcline, where the tangents are vertical.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3188/Source/Images/1e72cfbafb65f970d697dda4103fc713.png)\n\n- Next, the flow continues downward, but the trajectory starts to turn slightly to the left.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3188/Source/Images/77ad3d8eb22bdba17fcf14e48052927e.png)\n\n- After that, the trajectory continues to go downwards and slightly to the left to infinity.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3188/Source/Images/a442afec461d42b693fbda69339f3689.png)"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186164396 17592186164397 17592186164398 17592186164399 17592186164400] "immutable_fields" [[17592186164395 :answer-field/correct] [17592186164395 :answer-field/id] [17592186164395 :answer-field/key] [17592186164395 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
